sequenceDiagram
  participant C as Private Client
  participant S as LAN Switch
  participant N as Home NAT
  participant R as Internet Routers
  participant E as Server Edge Router
  participant P as Public Server

  Note over C,P: Outbound request
  Note over C: DNS resolved server to 203.0.113.20<br/>Application selected TCP port 443
  C->>S: Frame to gateway MAC<br/>IP flow 192.168.1.10:51514 to 203.0.113.20:443
  Note over S: Layer 2 state<br/>MAC table selects gateway port
  S->>N: Forward unchanged local frame
  Note over N: Create source NAT mapping<br/>192.168.1.10:51514 maps to 198.51.100.10:62000
  N->>R: Translated public flow<br/>198.51.100.10:62000 to 203.0.113.20:443
  Note over R: Each router performs longest prefix match<br/>Replaces Layer 2 header and decrements TTL<br/>Preserves public IP and port endpoints
  R->>E: Route toward server network
  Note over E: Connected route reaches final link<br/>ARP resolves server MAC
  E->>P: Final local frame to server MAC<br/>Public IP and port flow unchanged

  Note over C,P: Return response
  Note over P: Server sees client as<br/>198.51.100.10:62000
  P-->>E: Reply to public NAT endpoint<br/>203.0.113.20:443 to 198.51.100.10:62000
  Note over E: Server uses its local gateway MAC<br/>not the remote client MAC
  E-->>R: Route toward NAT public IP
  Note over R: Return path may differ<br/>Each router makes its own next hop decision
  R-->>N: Reply reaches 198.51.100.10:62000
  Note over N: NAT state finds private flow<br/>Reverse translate destination to 192.168.1.10:51514
  N-->>S: Private reply frame
  Note over S: MAC table selects client port
  S-->>C: Final frame to client MAC
